"Each fisher may harvest no more than 5% of the lake’s current fish stock per trip, and the community will maintain a mandatory 10% reserve of the current stock for future generations. Before each trip, all fishers record the lake’s estimated total weight on a shared ledger. The lake steward (a community‑appointed steward elected by simple majority each season) calculates 5% of that weight and announces the individual catch limit. Fishers must keep at least 1 kg for sustenance. Any fisher exceeding the limit will have the excess fish returned to the reserve and must pay a community fee equal to the excess weight in fish. The lake steward collects and holds this fee in a communal reserve account. Violations are logged and reviewed by a rotating community committee; a repeated infraction is defined as a third or more violation within a 12‑month period and results in a temporary fishing ban lasting until the next season. The reserve pool is replenished at the end of each season by returning 10% of the total catch to the lake. The rotating community committee is composed of fishers elected each month by a simple majority of those present; each fisher holds one vote. The committee convenes quarterly to review violation logs and may issue written bans, which the lake steward enforces by restricting access to fishing gear. The committee’s decisions are final unless overturned by a majority vote within the next month, in which case the steward will lift the ban."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may harvest no more than 5% of the lake’s current fish stock per trip, and the community will maintain a mandatory 10% reserve of the current stock for future generations. Before each trip, all fishers record the lake’s estimated total weight on a shared ledger. The lake steward (a community‑appointed steward elected by simple majority each season) calculates 5% of that weight and announces the individual catch limit. Fishers must keep at least 1 kg for sustenance. Any fisher exceeding the limit will have the excess fish returned to the reserve and must pay a community fee equal to the excess weight in fish. The lake steward collects and holds this fee in a communal reserve account. Violations are logged and reviewed by a rotating community committee; a repeated infraction is defined as a third or more violation within a 12‑month period and results in a temporary fishing ban lasting until the next season. The reserve pool is replenished at the end of each season by returning 10% of the total catch to the lake. The rotating community committee is composed of fishers elected each month by a simple majority of those present; each fisher holds one vote. The committee convenes quarterly to review violation logs and may issue written bans, which the lake steward enforces by restricting access to fishing gear. The committee’s decisions are final unless overturned by a majority vote within the next month, in which case the steward will lift the ban.","revised_operationalization":"1. Before each trip, the lake steward records the shared ledger entry of the estimated lake stock. 2. The steward calculates 5% of that number and posts the catch limit. 3. After the trip, the fisher records their catch, the reserve contribution, and any fee paid on the ledger. 4. The steward deposits the fee into the communal reserve account and records the amount. 5. Each month, the community gathers; fishers vote by simple majority to elect the members of the rotating committee. 6. The committee convenes quarterly to review violation logs, determine repeated infractions, and issue written bans. 7. The lake steward receives the ban notice and enforces it by denying the fisher access to shared fishing gear until the next season. 8. The committee’s decisions are final unless overturned by a majority vote within the next month, after which the steward lifts the ban. 9. At season’s end, 10% of the total catch is returned to the lake to replenish the reserve.","reasoning":"I added explicit details about how the rotating committee is elected, how it operates, and the steward’s role in enforcing bans so the proposal is complete and clear. The rest of the policy and operational steps stay the same, just with the added committee information to answer the question."}
